PHP @VERSION@ for OpenVMS @ARCH@ (vms-php, kit @KITVERSION@)
=====================================================================

PHP @VERSION@ built natively with VSI's clang (64-bit pointers, LP64), from the
signed php.net release with small, upstreamable patches (#ifdef __VMS). It runs
the phpBB 3.3 board in production behind lighttpd for OpenVMS.

  [VMSPHP.BIN]PHP.EXE       the command-line interpreter (CLI)
  [VMSPHP.BIN]PHP_CGI.EXE   CGI/FastCGI (run as a FastCGI server with -b)
  [VMSPHP.ETC]              PHP.INI (CLI), the -DIST templates, CA certificates
  [VMSPHP.COM]              VMSPHP$SETUP.COM (DCL symbols)
  [VMSPHP.DOC]              this file, licences, APACHE.MD, NEWS

Requirements: OpenVMS x86-64 (built and tested on E9.2-4), VSI SSL3
(OpenSSL 3.0) and TCP/IP Services.


1. After installation
---------------------

PRODUCT INSTALL VMSPHP runs VMSPHP$STARTUP INSTALL, which defines

  VMSPHP$ROOT      concealed, rooted at the installation (system, exec mode)
  USE_ZEND_ALLOC   "1": PHP's own memory manager on (see section 6)

and writes [ETC]PHP.INI from PHP.INI-DIST if there is none. To define both
at boot, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM, after TCP/IP and SSL3:

  $ @SYS$STARTUP:VMSPHP$STARTUP.COM

Remove any earlier definition of VMSPHP$ROOT first, and its DEFINE from the
startup procedures. VMSPHP$STARTUP defines it in executive mode, and an
ordinary DEFINE/SYSTEM (supervisor mode) is found before it, so PHP would
still run from the old tree:

  $ SHOW LOGICAL/FULL/ACCESS_MODE=SUPERVISOR VMSPHP$ROOT
  $ DEASSIGN/SYSTEM VMSPHP$ROOT

For the php and php_cgi symbols (LOGIN.COM or SYLOGIN.COM):

  $ @VMSPHP$ROOT:[COM]VMSPHP$SETUP
  $ php -v
  $ php "-r" "echo PHP_VERSION, PHP_EOL;"

DCL upper-cases unquoted arguments: quote options ("-r", "-d", "-c", "-T") and
anything case-sensitive.


2. Extensions
-------------

Built in (php -m): Core, ctype, date, dom, filter, hash, json, lexbor, libxml,
mbstring (without mbregex), mysqli, mysqlnd, openssl, pcre (PCRE2 10.49, no
JIT), PDO, pdo_mysql, pdo_sqlite, random, Reflection, session, SimpleXML,
SPL, sqlite3, standard, tokenizer, uri, xml, xmlreader, xmlwriter, zlib, and
Zend OPcache (no JIT).

Not built: gd (no image CAPTCHAs or thumbnails), intl, iconv, curl, sockets,
posix, pcntl, phar, ldap, PostgreSQL. dl() is not supported.

password_hash()/password_verify() support bcrypt and Argon2 (argon2i,
argon2id; the reference implementation is built in).

SQLite (sqlite3 and pdo_sqlite) is SQLite 3.54.1 from SQLite for OpenVMS
(vms-sqlite), built in. Its own file layer (VFS) makes a database safe to
share between processes and between accounts in different UIC groups, e.g.
a FastCGI pool and a CLI user:
- each database has a lock file beside it, <database>-lock, which SQLite
  never deletes: to delete a database, delete both (and any -journal);
- journals are rollback journals; PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL leaves the mode as
  it was (no WAL on OpenVMS);
- SQLite3::loadExtension() and PDO's loadExtension are not available;
- every account that writes the database needs write access to the file, its
  lock file and its directory (journals are created there), by UIC
  protection: PHP's is_writable(), which installers check, ignores ACLs.


3. Running PHP behind a web server (FastCGI)
--------------------------------------------

There is no fork on OpenVMS, so PHP_CGI.EXE runs as a FastCGI server, one
request at a time per process (PHP_FCGI_CHILDREN = 0), and the web server
spreads requests over several of them on consecutive ports:

  $ define/process PHP_FCGI_CHILDREN "0"
  $ php_cgi "-c" "/vmsphp_pool/php.ini" "-b" "127.0.0.1:19000"

Run each worker as a detached process (a DCL loop restarts it if it exits),
under the account that owns the application's writable files, with no
privileges beyond TMPMBX and NETMBX. lighttpd for OpenVMS's PHP_POOL.COM does
this (START, STOP, STATUS).

Start the pool's PHP.INI from VMSPHP$ROOT:[ETC]PHP-POOL.INI-DIST. It refers to
the pool directory through a rooted logical name, VMSPHP_POOL, with [.LOGS],
[.SESSIONS], [.TMP] and [.OPCACHE] beneath it. Those directories must be OWNED
by the pool's account (or accessible to it by UIC protection): PHP checks them
with access(), which ignores ACLs, and a worker whose opcache.file_cache it
cannot access exits at startup.

lighttpd: fastcgi.server with one ( "host" => "127.0.0.1", "port" => N )
entry per worker, "check-local" => "enable", "broken-scriptfilename" =>
"enable".

Apache (VSI APACHEWS 2.4): mod_proxy_fcgi and mod_proxy_balancer; see
[VMSPHP.DOC]APACHE.MD. (Not yet verified end to end.)


4. Applications: phpBB and others
---------------------------------

- Database host: use 127.0.0.1. ("localhost" also works with this build: it
  connects over TCP, as there are no usable Unix-domain sockets on VMS.)
- SQLite: phpBB 3.3 runs on it (database type "SQLite3"; put the database
  file outside the board's directory, in a directory the pool's account
  owns). See section 2 for sharing a database between accounts.
- Passwords hashed with argon2id by an earlier PHP (VSI's 8.1 did) keep
  working.
- phpBB's CAPTCHA: choose "Simple image" (ACP > Spambot countermeasures). The
  GD plugins need gd, which is not built; their images fail with HTTP 500.
- Outgoing HTTPS (phpBB's version check, remote avatars) verifies peers with
  the CA certificates in VMSPHP$ROOT:[ETC.CERTS] (Mozilla's root store as
  extracted by curl.se; openssl.capath, already set in the templates).
- Files PHP rewrites (fopen "w", file_put_contents) keep one version; rename()
  replaces the target's versions; unlink() removes every version.


5. Differences from PHP on Unix
-------------------------------

- No fork: proc_open(), pcntl and mail() are not available. Send mail over
  SMTP (phpBB's own SMTP client works with TCP/IP Services' SMTP server).
- exec(), system() and shell_exec() run DCL commands; exec()'s result code is
  the command's $STATUS (odd = 0, even = 1; C programs' exit(n) = n).
- Data written to an open file is seen by other opens after fflush() or
  fclose(); PHP's fflush() also syncs regular files.
- No hard links (link()); file times have no separate atime; chmod(f, 0)
  gives the default protection.
- readdir() returns "." and ".." like Unix (the C RTL alone does not).
- Paths: PHP uses UNIX-style names (/dev/dir/file, /logical/dir/file). File
  names with '*' cannot be created.
- AF_UNIX sockets and /tmp do not exist; sys_get_temp_dir() is /sys$scratch.
- time_t in the C RTL is 32-bit unsigned. PHP's own date functions are 64-bit
  (dates before 1970 and after 2038 work); file times (touch()) cannot be set
  before 1970.


6. USE_ZEND_ALLOC
-----------------

The C RTL's getenv() reads logical names, so a SYSTEM logical USE_ZEND_ALLOC
"0" (some earlier PHP installations define one) turns PHP's memory manager off
for every PHP process: slower, and memory_get_usage() returns 0.
VMSPHP$STARTUP defines it as "1". VSI's PHP 8.1 kit needs "0" and crashes in
its memory manager with "1": do not run both PHPs on one system.


7. Building from source
-----------------------

See https://github.com/issinoho/vms-php (tools/fetch.sh, prepare.sh,
build.sh; docs/ for the decisions and the porting log).
